{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pct-05-price-consumption-expenditure-revenue",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Price, Consumption, Expenditure, and Revenue",
    "description": "Keep expenditure fixed or track revenue when price and quantity change together, using the product relationship price × quantity.",
    "teachingApproach": "Anchor every problem to the product price × quantity. Use a ₹100-and-100-units model to derive inverse adjustments instead of asking learners to memorise a formula, then extend to same-money buying power and changing revenue.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 31,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 11, Percentage. Approved scope: Teach expenditure/revenue as price×quantity. Derive the chapter result that after a price increase of R%, consumption must fall by R/(100+R)×100% to keep expenditure fixed, and after a price decrease of R%, consumption may rise by R/(100−R)×100%. Cover same-money buying-power questions, price changes inferred from extra or fewer units bought, simultaneous price and quantity changes and their net effect on expenditure/revenue, and inverse percentage reasoning such as increasing speed to reduce travel time for the same distance. Include cases where expenditure is allowed to change by a stated percentage rather than remain exactly constant."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's connect three quantities that percentage questions often mix up:\n\nexpenditure = price × quantity.\n\nIf one factor rises and the total must stay fixed, the other factor has to move in the opposite direction.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose price is ₹100 per unit and you buy 100 units. Expenditure=₹10,000.\n\nIf price rises 25%, new price=₹125.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "To keep ₹10,000 fixed, quantity=10000/125=80 units.\n\nConsumption falls from 100 to 80: a 20% reduction.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
